00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:36.329
So if you're like me, you're probably wondering how this FaceApp program works.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:38.729
Now if you've been following this channel for the past few weeks at all

00:06:38.729 --> 00:06:43.200
You've probably seen the deep fakes that we've been working on. Deep fakes are basically a type of machine learning

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.380
Where you are passing an image into the computer

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:49.220
It's running that through its AI and learning on that image and then giving you an AI

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:54.540
Generated result that has been informed by the things that it learned on. You also may have seen style transfer.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:00.840
Style transfer is really cool. Style transfer basically determines which parts of your image are just textures and styles and then what do you need to

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:06.209
Be able to understand what that image is. Style transfer will basically break those two things apart, preserve the structure of your final image

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:11.190
But apply the style from a different image onto that. So you can make every picture look like Van Gogh's starry night.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.200
That's basically what you're getting with FaceApp.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.220
[Niko laughter]

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.300
It's really weird. But what I saw was just wrinkles everywhere.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.220
But it's like there's a pattern to it.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:25.199
The way this program is working is basically a mix of style transfer with some other structural cues.

00:07:25.199 --> 00:07:27.899
Kind of like how a deep fake works. If I want to make FaceApp

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:34.019
Basically, what I'd do, is I'd first program in a facial landmarks recognition system. The eyes are here. The nose is here.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.630
The mouth is here. And then I would train the computer on

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:42.659
Here's what various sets of wrinkles look like. Next to the eyes. Next to the nose. Next to the mouth. And then the computer can

00:07:42.659 --> 00:07:44.699
Be fed a new face with new landmarks

00:07:44.699 --> 00:07:48.989
And then go, "Okay, there should be wrinkles here, wrinkles here, wrinkles there. A mustache here. A beard there."

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:53.250
So I did a little experiment to see if I could kind of replicate this effect at very base level.

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:56.099
So I gave it this picture of a wrinkly old woman.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.169
So I took the style from this picture. Now it's just a picture.

00:07:58.169 --> 00:08:00.240
And I took a picture of myself and I applied

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:06.020
that picture of the wrinkly woman as the style on my face. And the wrinkles are there. The structure is actually there.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:08.580
Now the background has wrinkles too. It's all just become wrinkles.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:14.240
So if you were to then inform this with some facial landmarks, you kind of end up with what FaceApp is doing.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:18.640
It's doing a style transfer of the wrinkles it's learned from looking at hundreds of images old people.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:24.799
And it's doing a style transfer onto your skin. When you can combine a style transfer with recognizable landmarks

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.349
You can end up creating something like FaceApp.

